# `class A extends R { constructor(r) { super(r); this.w = 5; } }`, `class M extends A { constructor(r) { super(r); this.z = 2; } }`, `new M(r)` in a loop. A base constructor whose text only fills the object is copied
# into the class below. A derived one written by hand never was: its text holds the super call and the checks that stand round it. Called, it does not know its new.target: the prototype is asked of an operation, the
# object is made out of line below it, and it can never fall away.
# In a loop such a constructor is copied too where, beside the super call and those checks, its text only fills the object. What it calls above goes by the rules for that call.
# f1 = f, f2 = g . f3, f4, f6 = the constructors of R, A, M
# -- source: in-a-loop-a-derived-constructor-that-only-fills-the-object-behind-super-is-copied-into-the-class-below.cjs
